{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And from Bamoth to the valley.</b> From Bamoth one would descend to the mountain at the edge of the valley, and it extended along the top of the peak overlooking the edge of the wilderness — visible across the face of the desert, which is called Yeshimon, as it is written: ״ובתהו יליל ישימון״. And this was a great joy, that it was visible across the face of the desert to those traveling through the wasteland, for they would see a place to water themselves and their flocks, and to cool off and refresh themselves. And in Eruvin, chapter כיצד מערבין, it concludes: ״וממדבר מתנה״ — if a person makes himself like this desert, upon which everyone treads, his Torah endures, and it is given to him as a gift. And once it is given to him as a gift — ״ונחלו אל״, meaning it is to him like an inheritance. And once ״ונחלו אל״ — he rises to greatness, as it is said: ״ומנחליאל במות״. And if he grows arrogant, the Holy One, blessed be He, humbles him, as it is said: ״ומבמות הגיא״. And if he repents, the Holy One, blessed be He, exalts him, as it is said: ״כל גיא ינשא״."], :book :numbers, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:09:51.105086105Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}